if-else
“if else” statement: executes one block of code if a condition is met and a different block of code if it is not met.


x &lt;- rnorm(1)

if (x &gt; 0) {
  print("x is positive :)")
} else {
  print("x is negative :(")
}


Similarly, run it multiple times and annotate code with different colors: if-then syntax, logical condition, and what to do.

Nested ifs


x &lt;- rnorm(1)

if (x &gt; 0) {
  print("x is positive :)")
} else if (x &lt; 0) {
  print("x is negative :(")
} else {
  print("x is zero :|")
}


Run it multiple times, then get the zero to toggle using x &lt;- 0

if (bike_working) {
  print("ride bike")
} else if (enough_time) {
  print("ride bus")
} else {
  print("call cab")
}


If time you can set the values of bike_working and enough_time to verify that it works.

